# Reverse-dependency drop-in for docker.service (PR#13 round-8 finding
# 4354363965), installed and removed by role kanban_ui.
#
# Why this exists: Requires=/PartOf= on kanban-ui-docker-user.service only
# propagate stop/deactivation DOWN to the oneshot; systemd never activates
# a dependent back up when the required unit is (re)started. After a plain
# `systemctl stop docker && systemctl start docker` the oneshot stayed
# inactive (reproduced live: ExecMainStart unchanged, DOCKER-USER jump
# gone until the next playbook run), and BindsTo= did not fix it (same
# forward-only semantics). The activation edge must be a reverse
# dependency declared on the Docker side: Wants= pulls the oneshot into
# every docker start/restart transaction, and the oneshot's own
# After=docker.service (its unit file) supplies the ordering edge, so it
# runs after the daemon is ready — exactly when DOCKER-USER has just been
# rebuilt. Proven on synthetic units on the target host (systemd 249): without
# this drop-in the oneshot never re-fires across a daemon stop/start; with it,
# it re-fires every time.
#
# Wants= (not Requires=): a failing convergence script must never take the
# Docker daemon's activation down with it. This drop-in deliberately has
# NO After=/Before= of its own: the daemon must never gain an ordering
# edge onto the oneshot (that would be a start-order cycle against the
# oneshot's After=docker.service).
# Managed by Ansible (roles/kanban_ui); do not edit on the host.
[Unit]
Wants=kanban-ui-docker-user.service
